Except it's not, for the simple reason that you presume that an existing
feature (score) should be considered meaningless for the future from the
development perspective.

A feature (score) was added to the game originally.  The Dev Team
clearly intended score to matter, since it was a lot more effort to add
it than to leave it nonexistent.  An abuse now exists in the game that
renders score effectively meaningless.

Therefore, the "what-if" future case for the player who never cares
about score (doesn't use the feature) because he only ever plays on his
local copy is not useful to consider in determining whether or not the
bug should be fixed.  ("bugs", indeed, since there are other related
abuses -- but farming is the one that can be pulled off with the least
effort, and for that matter fixed with the least effort; my own change
required exactly two lines of code.)

> If you mean to say that scores are a major part of Nethack, 

They are, of course, a major feature.

> isn't *for me*. And what the Nethack user in general thinks is
> anyone's guess.

In general, a safe assumption is that the user's viewpoint can be
considered to be "I would like all features included with the game to be
at least working and/or useful as designed."

Currently score is unuseful for ascensions, and if you hung out in
#nethack on freenode you would see a _parade_ of new people who we have
to keep explaining this to; that score is a great measure of how close
you're getting to ascension, but not meaningful at all once you've
gotten there, and to quit worrying about getting a "high score" because
the top 10 spots (and hence, the graveyard) are already long taken by
farmers.  Their choices are either to farm themselves, or just accept
post-ascension score as meaningless and move on.

